{
  "id": "Compound::DB00741 - Disease::DOID:4481",
  "verbalization": "Hydrocortisone, a corticosteroid hormone receptor agonist, is implicated in the treatment of allergic rhinitis through its interaction with corticosteroid receptors. This relationship is supported by multiple pathways, where Hydrocortisone is directly linked to Asthma and Atopic Dermatitis, both of which are treated by Loratadine, a second-generation antihistamine. The shared mechanism of action involves the modulation of inflammatory pathways, with Hydrocortisone and Loratadine acting through similar receptor mechanisms to reduce inflammation and allergic responses. The lowest common ancestor of these relationships, the Carbonyl Compound, suggests a common biochemical pathway that may underlie their therapeutic effects. The evidence highlights a potential link between corticosteroid receptor activation and allergic rhinitis, emphasizing the role of receptor-based mechanisms in the treatment of inflammatory conditions.",
